  "content": {
    "title": "UCLA overpowers Minnesota to move to women's Elite Eight: Highlights",
    "text": "Updated March 27, 2026, 11:15 p.m. ETSACRAMENTO, CA – UCLA women's basketball is still dancing.The No. 1 Bruins defeated No. 4 Minnesota 80-56 in a wire-to-wire victory on Friday in the Sweet 16 round of the 2026 Women's NCAA Tournament in Sacramento, California. to punch their ticket to the Elite Eight for the second consecutive season. UCLA will face the winner of No. 2 LSU and No. 3 Duke in the Elite Eight.\"We are prepared to see who we will play,\" UCLA guard Kiki Rice said. \"But we are playing well, so whoever it is, we are going to be alright.\"Rice led the way for UCLA on Friday with a game-high 21 points against Big Ten rival Minnesota. Rice was one of four Bruins to score in double digits. Lauren Betts finished with 16 points, Angela Dugalic added a 13-point, 10-rebound double-double and Gabriela Jaquez had 10 points. UCLA dominated the paint, 52-22.Minnesota's Grace Grocholski had a team-high 12 points in the Golden Gophers' first Sweet 16 appearance since 2005.USA TODAY Sports provided live updates and highlights from Friday's matchup. Catch up on highlights from UCLA and Minnesota's Sweet 16 game: End of Q3: UCLA 53, Minnesota 41 UCLA led by as many as 17 points in the third quarter, but Minnesota closed the frame on a 6-2 run to come within 13. Kiki Rice exploded for eight of her team-high 14 points in the quarter. Lauren Betts has 12 points and Angela Dugalic has 10 points and seven rebounds. Minnesota’s Grace Grocholski is the lone Golden Gopher player in double digits with 10 points. No perfect March Madness brackets remainThere will be no perfect bracket this year. Hannah Hidalgo and Notre Dame finished off the last flawless entry with the upset win over Vanderbilt.Pittsburgh native Otto Schellhammer, “Oschell17” online, lasted six games longer than the next best bracket. There were over 5.2 million entries this year, but yet again, none will emerge free of faults.Better luck next year, folks. - Jack Little Lena Bilic knocks down 3-pointerUCLA started Friday’s Sweet 16 matchup against Minnesota 0-of-7 from the 3-point line, but Lena Bilic made the Bruins' first 3-pointer of the night with 4:15 remaining in the third quarter to go up 47-32. UCLA has double-digit lead vs. MinnesotaLauren Betts made a layup to extend UCLA’s lead to 13 points. Minnesota’s Grace Grocholski ended UCLA’s 8-0 run with a 3-pointer to cut the Golden Gophers' deficit to 10 points with 5:20 remaining in the third quarter. Kiki Rice converted two free throws to go up 12 points with 4:57 remaining, 44-32. UCLA goes on 4-0 runUCLA came out of the locker room determined and went on a 4-0 run, with all four points coming in the paint to extend the Bruins paint point advantage to 32-10. Minnesota head coach Dawn Plitzuweit called a timeout with 8:02 remaining in the third quarter as UCLA’s lead swelled to nine points, the largest of the game. Halftime: UCLA 34, Minnesota 29UCLA led by as many as eight points in the first quarter, but Minnesota outscored the Bruins 19-16 in the second quarter to cut its deficit to five points heading into halftime. The Bruins are dominating the paint 28-10, but struggling from deep. UCLA has not made a 3-pointer, 0-of-6 from the 3-point line. The Bruins are also getting outrebounded by Minnesota, 17-15.Lauren Betts (10) and Angela Dugalic (10) have combined for 20 of UCLA’s 28 points. Kiki Rice added six points. Sophie Hart and Tori McKinney each have six points for Minnesota. Grace Grocholski added five points and four rebounds. Angela Dugalic surpasses 1,000 career pointsWith a second-quarter layup, UCLA forward Angela Dugalic surpassed 1,000 career points.Dugalic transferred from Oregon in 2021. The graduate student didn’t take the court for the Bruins until February of that year following an injury in the preseason. She missed the entire 2022-23 campaign with injury. She has been a regular for the Bruins since returning in 2023-24. − Jack Little End of Q1: UCLA 18, Minnesota 10UCLA has an eight-point lead over Minnesota heading into the second quarter. Lauren Betts has a game-high six points and hasn't missed from the field, where she's a perfect 3-of-3. UCLA has a 12-6 edge in the paint and holds a slight advantage on the boards with 10 rebounds (three offensive), compared to eight for Minnesota. The Bruins have left some points on the board. They're shooting 40% from the free throw line (2-of-5). The team averages 78.3% from the line. UCLA burns challenge earlyUCLA head coach Cori Close challenged an out-of-bounds call on the Bruins with 5:23 remaining in the first quarter as UCLA led 6-4. Officials ruled the challenge unsuccessful and Minnesota kept possession of the ball. UCLA vs. Minnesota tips offThe Sweet 16 matchup between UCLA and Minnesota is underway at the Golden 1 Center in Sacramento, California. The Bruins have a 6-4 lead over the Golden Gophers with 5:23 remaining. Both teams traded buckets early on, but struggled to finish some shots at the rim, signaling some early nerves. UCLA is 3-of-9 from the field, while Minnesota is 2-of-5. UCLA's Lauren Betts has four points, one steal and block. 'Blanket Lady' attends UCLA-MinnesotaOn Friday morning, Minnesota Gov. Tim Walz issued a proclamation declaring March 27, 2026, as “Elvera ‘Peps’ Neuman, the Blanket Lady, Day” in Minnesota. If you’re watching the Sweet 16 matchup between No. 1 seed UCLA and No. 4 Minnesota, you’ll likely see her. Carrying a golden and maroon blanket, the “Blanket Lady,” hypes the Gopher crowd during the game by waving the blanket – and has since 2006. “It’s everything,” Neuman, 81, said about Minnesota basketball. “It’s my life.” Neuman is seated eight rows up from the court, opposite the Minnesota bench, for the Golden Gophers’ first Sweet 16 appearance in 21 years.Neuman has had season tickets since 2004. She hasn’t missed a home game in 15 years – even with a 126 mile round-trip commute. It’s all worth it, she said, through the snow, rain or sunshine. “She is such an encouragement,” fellow Minnesota fan Barbara Upenn said. “She is our shining star, and we just love her, and she brings so much enjoyment and joy to the entire game.”Neuman played basketball until age 44, after founding her own barnstorming team. She is a staple at sporting events across Minnesota.“She comes to every game,” fan Ramona Braun said. “She comes to every high school, the girls games, the boys games. She's at the volleyball games. She's everywhere.” − Wesley Webb What time is UCLA vs. Minnesota Sweet 16 game?The No. 1 UCLA Bruins will face off against the No. 4 Minnesota Golden Gophers in the Sweet 16 round of the 2026 Women's NCAA Tournament at 7:30 p.m. ET on Friday, March 27 in Sacramento, California.Date: Friday, March 27Time: 7:30 p.m. ETLocation: Golden 1 Center (Sacramento, CA)UCLA vs. Minnesota: TV, streamingTV: ESPNStream: ESPN Unlimited UCLA starting lineup1 Kiki Rice | G 5-11 - Senior5 Charlisse Leger-Walker | G 5-10 - Senior8 Gianna Kneepkens | G 6-0 - Senior11 Gabriela Jaquez | G 6-0 - Senior51 Lauren Betts | C 6-7 - Senior Minnesota starting lineup3 Amaya Battle | G 5-11- Senior10 Mara Braun | G 6-0 - Junior14 Tori McKinney | G 6-1 - Sophomore25 Grace Grocholski | G/F 5-10 - Junior52 Sophie Hart | G 6-5 - Senior When did UCLA play Minnesota in March Madness?The last time UCLA and Minnesota met in the NCAA Tournament was in 2004 when No. 7 Minnesota knocked off No. 10 UCLA before embarking on a stunning run to the Final Four.Minnesota was led by legendary guard Lindsay Whalen, often used as a measuring stick for current Minnesota women's basketball stars, including Amaya Battle.After beating UCLA in 2004, Minnesota upset No. 2 Kansas State, No. 3 Boston College and No. 1 Duke en route to becoming the first ever No. 7 seed in the Final Four. - Jack Little UCLA Bruins roster#NAMEPOSEXPHTHOMETOWN1Kiki RiceGSr.5' 11\"Bethesda, MD3Christina KaramouziGFr.5' 11\"Stockholm5Charlisse Leger-WalkerGSr.5' 10\"Waikato8Gianna KneepkensGSr.6' 0\"Duluth, MN